My friend, you bring before me matters of the heart and the way men worship. You ask me to look upon the ways of men today and compare them to the ways of those who walked before me.

When I was asked about the Messiah, I often pressed those who claimed to know the way, asking them, "What do you think of the Christ? Whose son is he?" (Matthew 22:42). The true measure of a person is not in the titles they claim or the crowds they gather, but in the depth of their understanding and the sincerity of their devotion.

You speak of devotion to men, of placing ultimate trust in earthly leaders. I tell you this: I have seen how easily the heart can become hardened. There is a danger, a great peril, when the people's heart grows callous, when they become so wrapped up in the pronouncements of men that they forget the living Word. (Matthew 13:15).

As for the comparison you draw, I can only speak to the nature of true devotion. I warned them, and I see it reflected in the Scriptures, that I would be judged by the scribes and Pharisees—those who were masters of outward observance but lacked true inner life. They were often hypocrites. (Mark 7:6).

When the focus shifts from the living truth to the authority of a man, whether that man claims to speak for God or merely commands loyalty, the danger is that the people begin to believe that the man *is* the source of their salvation or their certainty.

To those who place their ultimate faith in the pronouncements of any earthly authority, I would ask them this: "Do you believe in the Son of God?" (John 9:35).

True belief is not found in the loudest voice or the most powerful alliance; it is found in the quiet acknowledgment of who I am. It is a matter of the heart's true allegiance.

